m the party and yet  in terms of seats in terms of the wider  public it's not quite of course I was  desperately disappointed at the result  of the general election des really  disappointed its tanker own a virus to  wake everybody up to the value of what  we were saying then  and I think the future of the socialist  values that we put forward the future of  our party and its principles in the  community are as relevant and strong if  not stronger than ever let's move to  what it was like being leader for  decades you were a backbench  campaigner I've always been a campaign  campaigning politician always will be  what was it like to have to move from  that to becoming the Labour leader first  time I ever sat or spoke from the front  bench was when I became leader of the  party and I've never forgotten that  first Prime Minister's question time I  arrived there and the place was  absolutely RAM packed and all eyes were  on us and so I decided to ask questions  on behalf of the people that had sent  them in to me because I think politics  has to change and what I've learnt the  most is that you learn a lot actually  listening to people in in communities  all over the country there are a  thousand frustrations every day at being  leader of the party and there's never  enough hours in the day to achieve the  things that you want to do a thousand  frustrations every day what were your  biggest frustrations the difficulty of  getting getting a message across and  always having to balance whether you  respond to the immediate or try to raise  the sights into what you're trying to  achieve in the long term and dealing  with large personalities that the world  is full of what's been your deepest  regret not winning the two general  elections how was it when you lost that  second election was it crushing to be  defeated on that and on that scale it  must have been off of course it was a a  moment one's never going to forget but  you don't give up on your principles you  don't